Add browser-based VS Code workflow for rumpelpod

Add a VS Code extension for switching among pods with persistent agent
terminals and native review diffs. Include generated Rust-to-TypeScript
types, browser-mode development tooling, devcontainer services, and real
daemon browser integration coverage. Keep the browser server
unauthenticated and reachable only through loopback port forwarding.

Serve a separate anyhow demo repository so live test pods use a standard
nested runtime. Keep the demo beside the rumpelpod checkout under
`/workspaces` so it is easy to find from the development container.

Restore a Rumpelpod Activity Bar mode with the active agent rendered by
an extension-owned xterm.js terminal in the primary sidebar. Keep the
native review diff in the main editor and let VS Code choose inline or
side-by-side rendering. Preserve the compact pod switcher, creation and
shell controls, and event-driven status and review updates.

Keep pod selection available when another pod has a stale Git callback.
Only explicit refresh actions synchronize refs; picker and restoration
reads use the daemon's current inventory.

Always open a native review editor when selecting or restoring a pod.
Show an empty changes editor for clean pods instead of leaving the main
editor blank or displaying the previous pod's diff.

Make the active pod name the sidebar's pod selector and put its agent
and shell in extension-owned xterm tabs that keep both PTYs alive. Show
every changed file in one native vertically stacked VS Code review with
concise pod and file-path labels. Keep terminal focus intact when daemon
events refresh that review.
